WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:07.109
This is Anthropic, Read Aloud. Today: Deploying A I from pilot to production.

2
00:00:08.409 --> 00:00:10.622
Deploying A I from pilot to production.

3
00:00:11.072 --> 00:00:22.677
This guide by Anthropic and Accenture shares seven considerations for taking enterprise A I from pilot to production, and the decisions leadership needs to make before the program advances.

4
00:00:23.127 --> 00:00:27.308
From Claude Blog, published September 14, 2026.

5
00:00:28.308 --> 00:00:47.694
A successful A I pilot doesn’t guarantee successful deployment to the rest of an organization. According to Accenture’s Pulse of Change report (July 2026), only 23 percent of C-suite leaders report having achieved sustained, enterprise-wide impact with A I initiatives.

6
00:00:48.144 --> 00:01:10.028
Pilots are designed for success. Teams are handpicked, often selected for enthusiasm and capability, and work on a defined scope with clear timelines. Pilot budgets are often protected, with insulation from normal organizational dynamics. This makes pilots unrepresentative of the conditions under which A I programs run in production.

7
00:01:10.478 --> 00:01:32.942
Accenture’s September 2026 Tokenomics research found that 42 percent of organizations rely on shared IT and finance accountability, with no single owner responsible for A I costs and outcomes. Without that owner, it becomes difficult to measure success, make tradeoffs, and maintain accountability as an A I program scales.

8
00:01:33.392 --> 00:01:52.443
To help CIOs and technical leaders get A I programs from pilot into production, we worked with Accenture to put together a practical blueprint. It draws on what we’ve observed in enterprise deployments built on Claude and what Accenture has seen in the implementations it has guided across industries and geographies.

9
00:01:52.893 --> 00:01:54.109
In this guide, we share:

10
00:01:54.559 --> 00:02:02.340
Seven considerations to be settled in chronological order: before the pilot begins, during the pilot phase, and in production.

11
00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:13.342
At the end of each consideration, “work out” questions for the cross-functional team and the ownership decisions a CIO or business leader makes before the program advances.

12
00:02:13.582 --> 00:02:25.192
A four-part definition of the job the A I will do (user, task, output, and a measurable quality threshold), and a lightweight total cost of ownership model to build before the pilot.

13
00:02:25.432 --> 00:02:36.895
A four-tier oversight model (automated, sampled, reviewed, and advisory) that matches human review to the risk of each output, with example tasks and a review cadence for each tier.

14
00:02:37.135 --> 00:02:43.283
A transition blueprint laying out what to decide, when to decide it, and who needs to own each decision.

15
00:02:43.733 --> 00:02:44.660
Read the guide.

16
00:02:45.660 --> 00:03:02.423
